      {
        "id": "def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The geometric representation on the simple-root basis over a common splitting field, and the root set",
        "deps": [
          "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "def-rationals",
          "thm-rat-field",
          "def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring",
          "def-polynomials-that-split-and-splitting-fields",
          "thm-splitting-fields-exist-for-nonzero-polynomials",
          "cor-splitting-fields-exist-for-finite-families",
          "thm-polynomial-degree-of-a-product-over-a-domain",
          "thm-polynomial-ring-over-a-field-is-a-ufd",
          "def-ring-characteristic",
          "thm-characteristic-of-a-field-is-zero-or-prime",
          "thm-prime-subfield-classification",
          "def-roots-of-unity-in-a-field",
          "prop-the-roots-of-unity-in-a-field-form-a-finite-cyclic-group",
          "thm-separability-of-x-n-minus-one-and-the-order-of-the-group-of-roots-of-unity",
          "def-vector-space",
          "def-linear-basis",
          "def-linear-map",
          "def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map",
          "lem-composition-and-identity-linear-maps"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix and let $W$ be the group presented by it as in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]].\n\n**A common splitting field.** Put $h:=\\prod\\,(X^{2m(s,t)}-1)\\in\\mathbb{Q}[X]$, the product over all unordered pairs $\\{s,t\\}\\subseteq S$ with $s\\ne t$ and $m(s,t)<\\infty$ (an empty product when $\\#S\\le1$). Let $K$ be a splitting field of $h$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$ ([[thm-splitting-fields-exist-for-nonzero-polynomials]], [[cor-splitting-fields-exist-for-finite-families]], [[def-polynomials-that-split-and-splitting-fields]]). The prime subfield of $K$ is $\\mathbb{Q}$ ([[thm-prime-subfield-classification]], [[def-ring-characteristic]]), so $\\operatorname{char}K=0$ ([[thm-characteristic-of-a-field-is-zero-or-prime]], [[thm-rat-field]]); hence $X^n-1$ is separable over $K$ for every $n\\ge1$ and $\\mu_n(K)$ is cyclic of order $n$ ([[thm-separability-of-x-n-minus-one-and-the-order-of-the-group-of-roots-of-unity]], [[prop-the-roots-of-unity-in-a-field-form-a-finite-cyclic-group]]). **Fix** one primitive $2m(s,t)$-th root of unity $\\zeta_{st}\\in K$ for each unordered finite edge ([[def-roots-of-unity-in-a-field]]) and put\n$$c_{st}:=c_{ts}:=\\zeta_{st}+\\zeta_{st}^{-1}\\in K\\quad (m(s,t)<\\infty),\\qquad c_{st}:=c_{ts}:=2\\quad (m(s,t)=\\infty).$$\n\n**The representation on the simple-root basis.** Let $E$ be the $K$-vector space with basis $(\\alpha_s)_{s\\in S}$ ([[def-vector-space]], [[def-linear-basis]]). For each $s\\in S$ let $\\sigma_s:E\\to E$ be the unique $K$-linear map with\n$$\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)=-\\alpha_s,\\qquad \\sigma_s(\\alpha_t)=\\alpha_t+c_{st}\\alpha_s\\quad (t\\ne s).$$\nBy the basis computation of the strategy each $\\sigma_s$ is an involution and hence invertible ([[def-linear-map]], [[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]], [[lem-composition-and-identity-linear-maps]]).\n\n**The root set.** The **root set** of the construction is\n$$\\Phi:=\\{\\sigma_{s_1}\\sigma_{s_2}\\cdots\\sigma_{s_k}(\\alpha_t):k\\ge0,\\ s_1,\\dots,s_k,t\\in S\\}\\subseteq E,$$\nthe orbit of the simple roots under the group generated by the $\\sigma_s$ (each generator is an involution). Writing $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ for the representation supplied by [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], one has $\\Phi=\\{\\sigma_w(\\alpha_s):w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$, the $W$-orbit of the simple roots.\n\n**Conventions and limits.** (i) $c_{st}=c_{ts}$; $c_{st}=0$ exactly when $m(s,t)=2$; $c_{st}=2$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$; replacing $\\zeta_{st}$ by $\\zeta_{st}^{-1}$ leaves $c_{st}$ unchanged, so the construction depends on the chosen primitive roots only through the numbers $c_{st}$. (ii) No positivity, no integrality of roots over $\\mathbb{Z}$, no root-reflection identification, and no faithfulness or definiteness of any form is asserted; those are separate matters for later pages. (iii) $S$ is finite throughout, so the basis $(\\alpha_s)_{s\\in S}$ is finite and every linear map is specified by finitely many values.",
        "strategy": "This is a definition; the content is the construction, and each well-definedness obligation is closed by a published supplier or a two-line basis computation.\n\n1.1 The field and the constants exist. Each factor $X^{2m(s,t)}-1$ is nonzero, so their finite product $h$ is nonzero in the domain $\\mathbb{Q}[X]$ ([[thm-polynomial-degree-of-a-product-over-a-domain]], [[thm-polynomial-ring-over-a-field-is-a-ufd]], [[def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring]]); a common splitting field of a finite family is a splitting field of the product ([[thm-splitting-fields-exist-for-nonzero-polynomials]], [[cor-splitting-fields-exist-for-finite-families]]). $\\mathbb{Q}$ embeds in $K$ ([[def-rationals]], [[thm-rat-field]]), so the prime subfield of $K$ is $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $\\operatorname{char}K=0$ ([[thm-prime-subfield-classification]], [[def-ring-characteristic]], [[thm-characteristic-of-a-field-is-zero-or-prime]]). Hence $X^n-1$ is separable over $K$, $\\mu_n(K)$ is cyclic of order $n$, and primitive $n$-th roots exist for every $n$ ([[thm-separability-of-x-n-minus-one-and-the-order-of-the-group-of-roots-of-unity]], [[prop-the-roots-of-unity-in-a-field-form-a-finite-cyclic-group]], [[def-roots-of-unity-in-a-field]]). Selecting one primitive root per unordered finite edge is a finite selection; $\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$ is unchanged under $\\zeta\\mapsto\\zeta^{-1}$, so each $c_{st}$ is well defined once the choices are fixed, and $c_{st}=c_{ts}$ by construction. The special values: $c=0$ iff $\\zeta^2=-1$ iff $\\zeta$ has order $4$ iff $m=2$; and $c=2$ is assigned by definition when $m=\\infty$.\n\n2.1 The linear maps are well defined and invertible. Prescribing the images of a basis determines a unique $K$-linear map ([[def-linear-map]], [[def-linear-basis]]). Computing on the basis: $\\sigma_s^2(\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_s$ and, for $t\\ne s$, $\\sigma_s^2(\\alpha_t)=\\sigma_s(\\alpha_t+c_{st}\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_t+c_{st}\\alpha_s-c_{st}\\alpha_s=\\alpha_t$; hence $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$, each $\\sigma_s$ is its own inverse, and the orbit defining $\\Phi$ is a well-defined set ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]], [[lem-composition-and-identity-linear-maps]]).\n\n3.1 The representation and the orbit are supplied by the recorded justifier. The relators $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ and $(\\sigma_s\\sigma_t)^{m(s,t)}=\\mathrm{id}$ that make $s\\mapsto\\sigma_s$ descend to a homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ are proved in [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]]; after that lemma, $\\Phi$ is literally the $W$-orbit of the simple roots. Nothing about positivity, faithfulness, or a bilinear form is used here, so no such supplier is needed.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised book text, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154",
              "locator": "1.1 and Proposition 1.3, printed pp. 10-12: the space with basis (e_s) and the maps sigma_s(e_{s'}) = e_{s'} + 2 cos(pi/m_{s,s'}) e_s with 2 cos(pi/m) = zeta + zeta^{-1}"
            },
            {
              "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (Princeton University Press 2008; author's complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 6.12 and Appendix D.1, printed pp. 115-120 and 439-447: the geometric (Tits) representation fixed by a root basis; used here only for the constants convention"
            }
          ]
        },
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The rank-two block computation, exact dihedral orders, the signed reflection action, and ambient reducedness",
        "deps": [
          "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots",
          "def-linear-map",
          "def-linear-basis",
          "def-linear-subspace",
          "def-linear-combination-and-span",
          "def-internal-direct-sum",
          "def-dimension",
          "thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace",
          "def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum",
          "def-characteristic-polynomial-of-an-operator",
          "thm-eigenvectors-for-distinct-eigenvalues-are-linearly-independent",
          "cor-distinct-characteristic-roots-imply-diagonalisability",
          "def-order-in-a-group",
          "def-group",
          "def-group-power",
          "def-roots-of-unity-in-a-field",
          "prop-the-roots-of-unity-in-a-field-form-a-finite-cyclic-group",
          "def-divides-in-z",
          "def-natural-numbers",
          "def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map",
          "def-characteristic-polynomial-of-a-matrix",
          "lem-characteristic-polynomial-is-monic-and-has-extreme-coefficients",
          "thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis",
          "lem-direct-sum-criterion"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, and let $K$, $E$, $(\\alpha_s)_{s\\in S}$, the constants $c_{st}$ and the linear maps $\\sigma_s$ be as in [[def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots]]; let $W$ and $\\ell$ be as in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]. The involution, representation, signed-action and word assertions below hold for every finite $S$, including $S=\\varnothing$ and $\\#S=1$. Only the rank-two assertions require distinct generators.\n\n1. **Involutions.** $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $s\\in S$; each $\\sigma_s$ is invertible, and $\\ell(s)=1$.\n2. **The rank-two block.** For any distinct $s,t\\in S$, put $m:=m(s,t)$, $c:=c_{st}$, $P:=\\operatorname{span}(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)\\subseteq E$, $A:=\\sigma_s\\sigma_t$ and $B:=A|_P$. Then $A(P)\\subseteq P$, and in the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$,\n$$B(\\alpha_s)=(c^2-1)\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t,\\qquad B(\\alpha_t)=-c\\,\\alpha_s-\\alpha_t;\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(B)=c^2-2,\\qquad \\det(B)=1 .$$\nMoreover $A(v)-v\\in P$ for every $v\\in E$. Consequently $E=P\\oplus Q$ with $Q:=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_u:u\\in S,\\ u\\notin\\{s,t\\}\\}$, and for every $v=p+q$ with $p\\in P$, $q\\in Q$ and every $k\\ge1$,\n$$A^k(p+q)=B^k p+\\bigl(\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{k-1}\\bigr)\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q .$$\n3. **Exact order of the rank-two product.** For any distinct $s,t$ and the notation of (2), when $m<\\infty$ put $\\zeta:=\\zeta_{st}$, of order $2m$, so $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$; when $m=\\infty$ use $c=2$, with no $\\zeta$ stipulated. Then:\n   (a) if $2<m<\\infty$, the characteristic polynomial of $B$ is $X^2-(c^2-2)X+1=(X-\\zeta^2)(X-\\zeta^{-2})$ with distinct roots; $B$ is diagonalisable, $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$, $\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{m-1}=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for $0<k<m$;\n   (b) if $m=2$, then $c=0$ and $B=-\\mathrm{id}_P$;\n   (c) if $m=\\infty$, then $c=2$, $B\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$, $(B-\\mathrm{id}_P)^2=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for every $k\\ge1$.\n   Hence $\\sigma_s\\sigma_t=A$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$, and infinite order when $m=\\infty$; in particular $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for $0<k<m$ in the finite case.\n4. **The representation and the exact dihedral orders.** The assignment $s\\mapsto\\sigma_s$ respects every defining relator of $W$ and induces a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$. Consequently, for any distinct $s,t\\in S$, one has $s\\ne t$ in $W$ and $st$ has order exactly $m(s,t)$ in $W$ (infinite when $m(s,t)=\\infty$).\n5. **The signed reflection action.** Let $T:=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subseteq W$. For $s\\in S$ define $U_s:\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T\\to\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ by\n$$U_s(\\varepsilon,r):=\\bigl(\\varepsilon\\cdot(-1)^{\\delta(s,r)},\\ srs\\bigr),$$\nwhere $\\delta(s,r)=1$ if $r=s$ and $\\delta(s,r)=0$ otherwise. Then $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ for every $s$, and the assignment $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot s:=U_s(\\varepsilon,r)$ extends to a well-defined **right action** of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ (so $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot1=(\\varepsilon,r)$ and $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot(wv)=((\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w)\\cdot v$). Indeed the only relations to check are $s^2=1$ and, for distinct $s,t$ with $m=m(s,t)<\\infty$, $(st)^m=1$: $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ because for $r=s$ the two signs cancel while for $r\\ne s$ one has $srs\\ne s$ and $U_s(\\varepsilon,srs)=(\\varepsilon,r)$; and the alternating word $s\\,t\\,s\\,t\\cdots$ of length $2m$ acts trivially because its $2m$ prefix reflections $r_i:=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$ (with $w_j:=s_1\\cdots s_j$) satisfy $r_{i+m}=r_i$ for $1\\le i\\le m$ and $r_1,\\dots,r_m$ are pairwise distinct, so each reflection of the dihedral subgroup $\\langle s,t\\rangle$ occurs exactly twice and every accumulated sign is even while the conjugating coordinate returns to $r$ because $(st)^m=1$.\n6. **Prefix reflections, deletion and expression independence.** Let $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ be a word in $S$ with value $w=s_1\\cdots s_k$ and prefix reflections $r_i=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1$, and let $n(r):=\\#\\{i:r_i=r\\}$.\n   (a) If $r_i=r_j$ for some $i<j$, then $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=w$: the two letters $s_i,s_j$ can be deleted.\n   (b) $(-1)^{n(r)}=:\\eta(r,w)$ depends only on $w$ and $r$, and the right action satisfies $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w=(\\varepsilon\\,\\eta(r,w),\\,w^{-1}rw)$.\n   (c) If the word is reduced ($k=\\ell(w)$), then $n(r)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ for every $r$, the map $i\\mapsto r_i$ is injective, and the set $\\Phi(w):=\\{r_1,\\dots,r_k\\}=\\{r\\in T:\\eta(r,w)=-1\\}$ is independent of the reduced expression chosen, with $\\#\\Phi(w)=\\ell(w)$.\n7. **Ambient reducedness of dihedral words.** Let $s\\ne t$, $m:=m(s,t)$, and for $q\\ge1$ let $w_q$ be the value of the alternating word of length $q$ beginning with $s$. If $m<\\infty$ and $q\\le m$, or if $m=\\infty$ and $q\\ge1$, then $\\ell(w_q)=q$, every word in $S$ representing $w_q$ has length at least $q$, and $w_1,\\dots,w_q$ are pairwise distinct. Thus dihedral alternating words are reduced in the ambient group $W$, not merely inside $\\langle s,t\\rangle$.",
        "strategy": "1.1 **Involutions and nonidentity for every generator.** Fix any $s\\in S$. The primitive formulas of [F2] give $\\sigma_s^2(\\alpha_s)=\\sigma_s(-\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_s$ and, for $u\\ne s$, $\\sigma_s^2(\\alpha_u)=\\sigma_s(\\alpha_u+c_{su}\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_u+c_{su}\\alpha_s-c_{su}\\alpha_s=\\alpha_u$. By [F3] the square is the identity on $E$, so $\\sigma_s$ is invertible with inverse itself. Also $\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)=-\\alpha_s\\ne\\alpha_s$, since the basis vector $\\alpha_s$ is nonzero and $\\operatorname{char}K=0$; hence $\\sigma_s\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$. These computations require no second generator; when $S=\\varnothing$, the assertions indexed by $s$ are vacuous.\n\n1.2 **The rank-two block.** Fix any distinct $s,t\\in S$ and use the notation of (2). Since $\\sigma_t(\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t$ and $\\sigma_t(\\alpha_t)=-\\alpha_t$ with $c=c_{st}$, linearity of $\\sigma_s$ gives $A(\\alpha_s)=\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t)=(c^2-1)\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t$ and $A(\\alpha_t)=\\sigma_s(-\\alpha_t)=-c\\,\\alpha_s-\\alpha_t$, so $A(P)\\subseteq P$ and the matrix of $B=A|_P$ in the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$ is $\\begin{pmatrix}c^2-1&-c\\\\ c&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, whose trace is $(c^2-1)+(-1)=c^2-2$ and whose determinant is $(c^2-1)(-1)-(-c)c=1$. For $u\\notin\\{s,t\\}$ one has $A(\\alpha_u)=\\sigma_s(\\alpha_u+c_{tu}\\alpha_t)=\\alpha_u+(c_{su}+c_{tu}c)\\alpha_s+c_{tu}\\alpha_t$, so $A(\\alpha_u)-\\alpha_u\\in P$; by linearity $A(v)-v\\in P$ for every $v\\in E$.\n\n2.1 **The two-block recursion.** The subspace $Q=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_u:u\\notin\\{s,t\\}\\}$ satisfies $P\\cap Q=\\{0\\}$ and $P+Q=E$ because the basis of $E$ splits into the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$ of $P$ and the basis of $Q$; hence $E=P\\oplus Q$, and every $v\\in E$ has unique coordinates $v=p+q$ with $p\\in P$, $q\\in Q$. I claim that for every $k\\ge1$ and all such $p,q$ one has $A^k(p+q)=B^k p+S_k\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q$, where $S_k:=\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{k-1}$. For $k=1$ this reads $A(p)+A(q)=Bp+(A(q)-q)+q$, which holds because $A|_P=B$. If it holds for $k$, then $B^kp+S_k(A(q)-q)\\in P$ and $A(q)-q\\in P$ by step 1.2, so applying $A$ and using $A|_P=B$ gives $A^{k+1}(p+q)=B\\bigl(B^kp+S_k(A(q)-q)\\bigr)+A(q)=B^{k+1}p+\\bigl(BS_k+\\mathrm{id}_P\\bigr)\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q$, and $BS_k+\\mathrm{id}_P=B+B^2+\\cdots+B^k+\\mathrm{id}_P=S_{k+1}$, which completes the induction.\n\n2.2 **The cases $m=2$ and $m=\\infty$.** If $m=2$, then $\\zeta$ has order $4$, so $\\zeta^2=-1$ and $\\zeta^{-1}=-\\zeta$, whence $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}=0$; substituting into the matrix of step 1.2 leaves $B=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\\\ 0&-1\\end{pmatrix}=-\\mathrm{id}_P$, so $B^2=\\mathrm{id}_P$ and $\\mathrm{id}_P+B=0$. If $m=\\infty$, then $c=2$ by the convention in [F2], and the matrix of step 1.2 gives $B=\\mathrm{id}_P+M$ with $M=B-\\mathrm{id}_P=\\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\\\ 2&-2\\end{pmatrix}\\ne0$ and $M^2=0$. The binomial expansion in the commutative algebra of endomorphisms then gives $B^k=(\\mathrm{id}_P+M)^k=\\mathrm{id}_P+kM$ for every $k\\ge1$, and $kM\\ne0$ because $M\\ne0$ and $k\\cdot1_K\\ne0$ in the characteristic-zero field $K$; hence $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for all $k\\ge1$, and in particular $B\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$.\n\n2.3 **The finite case $2<m<\\infty$.** By step 1.2 and [F5] the characteristic polynomial of $B$ is $\\chi_B(x)=x^2-(c^2-2)x+1$; since $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$ satisfies $c^2-2=\\zeta^2+\\zeta^{-2}$ and $\\zeta^2\\zeta^{-2}=1$, one has $\\chi_B(x)=x^2-(\\zeta^2+\\zeta^{-2})x+1=(x-\\zeta^2)(x-\\zeta^{-2})$. The two roots are distinct: $\\zeta^2=\\zeta^{-2}$ would give $\\zeta^4=1$, impossible because $\\zeta$ has order $2m>4$. Hence the characteristic polynomial splits into distinct linear factors, and [F6] makes $B$ diagonalisable, with eigenvalues $\\zeta^2$ and $\\zeta^{-2}$. Consequently $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$, because on an eigenvector for $\\lambda\\in\\{\\zeta^2,\\zeta^{-2}\\}$ the operator $B^m$ scales it by $\\lambda^m=\\zeta^{\\pm2m}=1$ by [F7] and [F8]; the operator $S_m=\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{m-1}$ is zero, because the polynomial $1+x+\\cdots+x^{m-1}$ vanishes at both eigenvalues, where $\\zeta^{\\pm2m}=1$ and $\\zeta^{\\pm2}\\ne1$ since $0<2<2m$; and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for $0<k<m$, because on the eigenvector for $\\zeta^2$ it scales by $\\zeta^{2k}\\ne1$, again since $0<2k<2m$ and $2m$ is the least positive exponent killing $\\zeta$.\n\n3.1 **Order of $A$, the relators, and the exact dihedral orders.** For any distinct pair $s,t$ with finite $m=m(s,t)$, step 2.1 gives $A^m(p+q)=B^mp+S_m(A(q)-q)+q=p+q$ for all $p,q$, using $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$ and $S_m=0$ from steps 2.2 and 2.3; hence $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$. If $0<k<m$, choose $p\\in P$ with $B^kp\\ne p$, which exists because $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$; then $A^k(p)=B^kp\\ne p$, so $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A$ has order exactly $m$. For $m=\\infty$, $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for every $k\\ge1$ gives $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $k\\ge1$, so $A$ has infinite order. Since this verifies each arbitrary distinct finite pair and step 1.1 proves $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every generator, the map $f:S\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$, $f(s)=\\sigma_s$, satisfies the hypotheses of the universal property in [F1], so there is a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$ for all $s$. For $\\#S\\le1$ there are no distinct-pair relators, so the same universal property applies using only step 1.1; for $S=\\varnothing$ it gives the unique homomorphism from the trivial group. For every $s\\in S$, step 1.1 now gives $\\sigma(s)\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$, hence $s\\ne1$ and $\\ell(s)\\ge1$ by [F1]; the one-letter expression gives $\\ell(s)\\le1$, so $\\ell(s)=1$. If $s\\ne t$ then $\\sigma_s\\ne\\sigma_t$, because $\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)=-\\alpha_s$ while $\\sigma_t(\\alpha_s)=\\alpha_s+c_{st}\\alpha_t$ and $-2\\alpha_s-c_{st}\\alpha_t\\ne0$ by linear independence of $\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t$ and $2\\ne0$ in $K$; hence $s\\ne t$ in $W$. Finally, $st$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$: writing $n$ for the order of $st$ in $W$, one has $n\\le m$ because $(st)^m=1$ in $W$ and $n$ is least, and $m\\le n$ because $A^n=\\sigma((st)^n)=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $m$ is the least positive exponent killing $A$; hence $n=m$. When $m=\\infty$, a positive exponent $k$ with $(st)^k=1$ would give $A^k=\\mathrm{id}_E$, so no such exponent exists and $st$ has infinite order.\n\n4.1 **The signed action.** First, $U_s$ is a bijection of $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ with $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$: for $r=s$ one has $U_s(\\varepsilon,s)=(-\\varepsilon,s)$ and applying $U_s$ again gives $(+\\varepsilon,s)$, while for $r\\ne s$ one has $srs\\ne s$ — if $srs=s$ then multiplying on the left by $s$ gives $rs=1$ and hence $r=s^{-1}=s$ using $s^2=1$ in $W$ — so $\\delta(s,srs)=0$ and the two applications of $U_s$ return $(\\varepsilon,r)$ from $U_s(\\varepsilon,srs)=(\\varepsilon,r)$. Next, fix a word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ in $S$ with $w_j:=s_1\\cdots s_j$ and prefix reflections $r_i:=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$; applying $U_{s_1},U_{s_2},\\dots,U_{s_k}$ successively to $(\\varepsilon,r)$ returns $\\bigl(\\varepsilon(-1)^{n(r)},\\,w_k^{-1}rw_k\\bigr)$ with $n(r):=\\#\\{i:r_i=r\\}$, by induction on $k$: the $i$-th step multiplies the sign by $(-1)^{\\delta(s_i,\\,w_{i-1}^{-1}rw_{i-1})}$, whose exponent is $1$ exactly when $r=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}=r_i$, and it conjugates the coordinate to $s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}rw_{i-1}s_i=(w_{i-1}s_i)^{-1}r(w_{i-1}s_i)$. Now fix any distinct $a,b\\in S$ with $m=m(a,b)<\\infty$; consider the alternating word $(a,b,a,b,\\dots)$ of length $2m$ and write $r_i=(ab)^{i-1}a$ for $1\\le i\\le 2m$ for its prefix reflections. The closed form is proved by induction on $i$: the conjugating prefix is $w_{i-1}=(ab)^{(i-1)/2}$ for odd $i$ and $w_{i-1}=(ab)^{(i-2)/2}a$ for even $i$, and the identities $(ab)^{-j}=(ba)^j$ and $a(ba)^j=(ab)^ja$ rewrite $w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$ as $(ab)^{i-1}a$ in both cases. Hence $r_{i+m}=(ab)^{i-1}(ab)^ma=(ab)^{i-1}a=r_i$ for $1\\le i\\le m$, while $r_1,\\dots,r_m$ are pairwise distinct: an equality $r_i=r_j$ with $i<j\\le m$ gives $(ab)^{j-i}=1$ after right multiplication by $a$, contradicting $0<j-i<m$ and the fact from step 3.1 that $ab$ has order exactly $m$. Therefore the sequence of $2m$ prefix reflections is $r_1,\\dots,r_m,r_1,\\dots,r_m$, so $n(r)\\in\\{0,2\\}$ for every $r\\in T$ and every accumulated sign $(-1)^{n(r)}$ is $1$; the conjugating coordinate returns to $r$ because $w_{2m}=(ab)^m=1$. So the permutation effected by each alternating word of length $2m$ is the identity, which verifies $(U_aU_b)^m=\\mathrm{id}$ and $(U_bU_a)^m=\\mathrm{id}$ for this arbitrary finite pair. There are no distinct-pair relators when $\\#S\\le1$, so the same verification applies in those cases using only $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$. By [F1]'s universal property applied to $f:S\\to\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T)$, $f(s)=U_s$, there is then a homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T)$ with $\\rho(s)=U_s$ for all $s$. Define $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w:=\\rho(w^{-1})(\\varepsilon,r)$; this is a right action, because $(wv)^{-1}=v^{-1}w^{-1}$ gives $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot(wv)=\\rho(v^{-1})\\bigl(\\rho(w^{-1})(\\varepsilon,r)\\bigr)=((\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w)\\cdot v$, and $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot s=\\rho(s)^{-1}(\\varepsilon,r)=U_s(\\varepsilon,r)$.\n\n5.1 **Deletion.** Suppose $r_i=r_j$ for some $i<j$, that is $w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}=w_{j-1}s_jw_{j-1}^{-1}$. Multiplying on the left by $w_{i-1}^{-1}$ and on the right by $w_{j-1}$ and using $w_{i-1}^{-1}w_{j-1}=s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}$ gives $s_i\\,s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}=s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}s_j$, hence $s_i s_{i+1}\\cdots s_j=s_{i+1}\\cdots s_{j-1}$. Substituting this into the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ shows that deleting the two letters $s_i$ and $s_j$ leaves the value unchanged: $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=w$.\n\n6.1 **Expression independence and reduced words.** For a word of $w$ the sign accumulated in step 4.1 is $(-1)^{n(r)}$, and the right action $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w$ is independent of the word; comparing with the displayed formula for two words of $w$ shows that $(-1)^{n(r)}=\\eta(r,w)$ depends only on $w$ and $r$, and that $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w=(\\varepsilon\\,\\eta(r,w),\\,w^{-1}rw)$ for every word of $w$. If the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ is reduced, then $n(r)\\le1$ for every $r$: if $n(r)\\ge2$, two indices would have $r_i=r_j$ with $i<j$, and step 5.1 would produce a word of length $k-2$ for $w$, contradicting $k=\\ell(w)$. Consequently the map $i\\mapsto r_i$ is injective, so the set $\\{r_1,\\dots,r_k\\}=\\{r\\in T:\\eta(r,w)=-1\\}$ has exactly $k=\\ell(w)$ elements; by the independence of $\\eta$ from the word, this set is independent of the reduced expression chosen.\n\n7.1 **Ambient reducedness of alternating words.** Let $s\\ne t$, $m:=m(s,t)$, and let $w_q$ be the value of the alternating word $(s,t,s,\\dots)$ of length $q$ beginning with $s$, where $q\\le m$ if $m<\\infty$ and $q\\ge1$ is arbitrary if $m=\\infty$. Its prefix reflections are $r_i=(st)^{i-1}s$ by the closed form of step 4.1. They are pairwise distinct: an equality $r_i=r_j$ with $i<j\\le q$ gives $(st)^{j-i}=1$ after right multiplication by $s$, which is impossible when $m<\\infty$ because then $0<j-i<q\\le m$ and $m$ is the least positive exponent killing $st$ by step 3.1 and [F7], and equally impossible when $m=\\infty$ because by step 3.1 no positive power of $st$ is $1$. Hence exactly $q$ elements $r$ satisfy $\\eta(r,w_q)=-1$, namely the $r_i$; on the other hand, for any word of length $k$ in $S$ representing $w_q$ [F1], step 6.1 gives $\\#\\{r:\\eta(r,w_q)=-1\\}=\\#\\{r:n'(r)\\text{ odd}\\}\\le\\sum_r n'(r)=k$, where $n'(r)$ counts the occurrences of $r$ among that word's prefix reflections. Therefore $k\\ge q$ for every word for $w_q$, so $\\ell(w_q)=q$; in particular the values $w_1,\\dots,w_q$ are pairwise distinct, having distinct lengths.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised book text, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154",
              "locator": "Propositions 1.3-1.7 and Corollary 1.4, printed pp. 11-13: the rank-two characteristic polynomial, the exact order of ss', the action U_s(eps,t)=(eps(-1)^{delta(s,t)},sts), the k-th term identity behind the sign count, and two-letter deletion"
            },
            {
              "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (Princeton University Press 2008; author's complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 3.1 and Lemmas 3.2.6, 3.3.5, printed pp. 26-39: dihedral analysis, prefix reflections, two-letter deletion and the sign-counted action on R x {+-1}"
            }
