  {
    "order": 1709,
    "id": "coxeter-presentations-exchange-and-reduced-word-theorems-examples",
    "kind": "B",
    "category": "coxeter-groups",
    "title": "Coxeter Presentations, Exchange, and Reduced Word Theorems — Examples",
    "companion": "coxeter-presentations-exchange-and-reduced-word-theorems",
    "requires": [
      "coxeter-presentations-exchange-and-reduced-word-theorems"
    ],
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "ex-hh-rank-one-reduced-words",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "Reduced words in rank one",
        "deps": [
          "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots",
          "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $S=\\{s\\}$ and $m(s,s)=1$, so that the relator set of the presentation of\n[[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]] is $R=\\{s^2\\}$ and\n$W=F(\\{s\\})/\\langle\\!\\langle s^2\\rangle\\!\\rangle$. Then:\n\n1. Every element of $W$ is $1$ or $s$, with $s^2=1$ and $s\\ne1$; hence\n$W\\cong\\mathbb{Z}/2$.\n2. $\\ell(1)=0$ and $\\ell(s)=1$.\n3. A word $(s,\\dots,s)$ of length $k$ has value $s^k$, which is $1$ for even\n$k$ and $s$ for odd $k$. Hence the only reduced words are the empty word\n$(\\;)$ for $1$ and the word $(s)$ for $s$, and every word of length $k\\ge2$ is\nnonreduced and reduces to a reduced word by repeated deletion of two letters.\n4. The reflection set is $T=\\{s\\}$, the root set is\n$\\Phi=\\{\\alpha_s,-\\alpha_s\\}$ (because $\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)=-\\alpha_s$), and the\nsigned action on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times\\{s\\}$ is $(\\varepsilon,s)\\cdot s=(-\\varepsilon,s)$,\nwhich is faithful.",
        "strategy": "1.1 **The group and its two elements.** The relation $s^2=1$ holds in $W$ because $s^2\\in R\\subseteq N$ [F1]. Every element of $W$ is the image of a product of the generator $s$ and its inverse, and $s^{-1}=s$ in $W$, so every element is a power $s^k$ with $k\\ge0$; since $s^k$ is $1$ for even $k$ and $s$ for odd $k$, one has $W=\\{1,s\\}$. By [F1] $\\ell(1)=0$ and by [F3] $\\ell(s)=1$, so $s\\ne1$, and the bijection $W\\to\\mathbb{Z}/2$ with $1\\mapsto0$, $s\\mapsto1$ is a homomorphism; hence $W\\cong\\mathbb{Z}/2$.\n\n2.1 **Word values and reduced words.** A word $(s,\\dots,s)$ of length $k$ has value $s^k$, which is $1$ for even $k$ and $s$ for odd $k$ by step 1.1; in particular $(\\;)$ has value $1$ and $(s)$ has value $s$ with lengths $0=\\ell(1)$ and $1=\\ell(s)$ [F1, F3], so both are reduced. If $k\\ge2$, the word of length $k$ has value of length at most $1<k$, so it is not reduced; by [F4] it admits a deletion of two letters with the same value, and iterating this deletion, the length drops by two each time until the word has length $\\ell(1)$ or $\\ell(s)$, namely until it is $(\\;)$ or $(s)$. Hence the only reduced words are $(\\;)$ and $(s)$.\n\n3.1 **Reflections, roots and the signed action.** Since $W$ is abelian and $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$, the reflection set is $T=\\{s\\}$ [F3, step 1.1]. By [F3] the group generated by $\\sigma_s$ is $\\{1,\\sigma_s\\}$, so the root set of [F2] is $\\Phi=\\{\\alpha_s,\\sigma_s(\\alpha_s)\\}=\\{\\alpha_s,-\\alpha_s\\}$, and these two vectors are distinct: $\\alpha_s=-\\alpha_s$ would give $2\\alpha_s=0$, while the basis vector $\\alpha_s$ is nonzero and $\\operatorname{char}K=0$ [F2]. For $r=s$ the formula of [F3] gives $(\\varepsilon,s)\\cdot s=U_s(\\varepsilon,s)=(\\varepsilon(-1)^{\\delta(s,s)},sss)=(-\\varepsilon,s)$, and this action is faithful by [F4].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised book text, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154",
              "locator": "1.1-1.3, printed pp. 10-12: the rank-one case of the Coxeter matrix, the sign character and l(s)=1"
            },
            {
              "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (Princeton University Press 2008; author's complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 3.1 and Example 3.2.4, printed pp. 26-31: dihedral groups and the set of conjugates of S"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "Reduced words and lengths in a finite dihedral group",
        "deps": [
          "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $m\\ge2$, $S=\\{s,t\\}$ and $m(s,t)=m$, so that $W=\\langle s,t\\mid s^2=t^2=(st)^m=1\\rangle$ is dihedral of order $2m$ by [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] (4).\n\n1. The $2m$ elements $(st)^k$ and $(st)^ks$ with $0\\le k<m$ are pairwise distinct and exhaust $W$.\n2. For $1\\le q\\le m$ the alternating words of length $q$ are reduced and represent distinct elements; explicitly, the element represented by the alternating word $s\\,t\\,s\\cdots$ of length $q$ has length $q$.\n3. For $0\\le k\\le m$ one has\n$$\\ell\\bigl((st)^k\\bigr)=\\min(2k,\\,2(m-k)),$$\nand for $0\\le k\\le m-1$ one has $(st)^ks=(ts)^{m-k}s=(ts)^{m-k-1}t$ and\n$$\\ell\\bigl((st)^ks\\bigr)=\\min(2k+1,\\,2(m-k)-1);$$\nin particular $\\ell(s)=1$ and $\\ell\\bigl((st)^{m-1}s\\bigr)=1$.\n4. The element $(st)^k$ with $2k=m$ (only for even $m$) is the unique longest element $w_0$ of $W$, of length $m$; its two reduced expressions are the two alternating words of length $m$, and they are related by the braid move $stst\\cdots\\mapsto tsts\\cdots$.",
        "strategy": "All claims are finite computations in the dihedral group.\n\n1.1 Elements. By part 4 of [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] the subgroup $\\langle s,t\\rangle=W$ has order exactly $2m$ and is dihedral; its elements are the rotations $(st)^k$ and the reflections $(st)^ks$ for $0\\le k<m$, and distinctness follows from the exact order of $st$ ([[def-order-in-a-group]]).\n2.1 Reduction and lengths. A word over $\\{s,t\\}$ with a repeated consecutive letter is nonreduced (deleting the pair shortens it, part (3) of [[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]]), and deleting two letters from an expression never increases the possible length; iterating, every word reduces to an alternating word. The alternating word of length $q\\le m$ is reduced with length $q$ and its value has no shorter expression by part (7) of [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] (ambient reducedness); two different alternating words of length $\\le m$ represent distinct elements because their values have different lengths or are the two distinct elements of length $m$ (part 1).\n3.1 The formulae. $(st)^k=(ts)^{m-k}$ because $(ts)=(st)^{-1}$ and $(st)$ has order $m$ ([[def-group-power]]); the shorter of the two alternating words $2k$ and $2(m-k)$ is reduced by 2.1, and no shorter word exists by part (7) of [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], so $\\ell((st)^k)=\\min(2k,2(m-k))$. Similarly $(st)^ks=(ts)^{m-k}s=(ts)^{m-k-1}t$ for $k\\le m-1$ (the last step cancels the final pair $ss$ of the literal product $(ts)^{m-k}s=(ts)^{m-k-1}tss$), and the reduced alternating word has the length $\\min(2k+1,2(m-k)-1)$; the endpoint $k=0$ gives $\\ell(s)=1$ and $k=m-1$ gives $\\ell((st)^{m-1}s)=1$. The longest element statement is the case $2k=m$ of the first formula together with 2.1: $w_0=(st)^{m/2}$ has the two alternating reduced words and its two expressions differ by one braid move ([[thm-hh-matsumoto-reduced-word-theorem]] (1) specialised to the rank-two case).",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised book text, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154",
              "locator": "Corollary 1.4, printed p. 12: the dihedral subgroup consists of the elements 1_k and 2_k (k=0,...,m), distinct except 1_0=2_0 and 1_m=2_m, with l(1_k)=l(2_k)=k"
            },
            {
              "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (Princeton University Press 2008; author's complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 3.1, printed pp. 26-29: the explicit structure and normal form of a finite dihedral group"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4
