      {
        "id": "ex-hh-exchange-deletion-on-a-nonreduced-word",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "A nonreduced word deleted by its repeated prefix reflection, and an exchange step",
        "deps": [
          "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
          "ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $S=\\{s,t\\}$ with $m(s,t)=3$, so that $W=I_2(3)$ is the dihedral group of order $6$ in which $st$ has order $3$.\n\n1. **Exchange.** The word $(s,t)$ is a reduced expression of $st$ with $\\ell(st)=2$. Since $s\\cdot st=t$ has length $1<2$, the exchange theorem ([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (2)) predicts $s\\cdot st=t=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_1}\\cdots s_2$, the deletion of the first letter; and indeed $s\\,st=t$.\n2. **A nonreduced word.** In the word $(s,t,s,t)$ the prefix reflections are $r_1=s$, $r_2=sts$, $r_3=t$, $r_4=s$; thus $r_1=r_4$. Deleting the first and the last letter gives the word $(t,s)$ with value $ts$, and indeed $stst=(st)^2=(st)^{-1}=ts$, so the deleted word is an expression of the same element: $stst=ts$.\n3. **Consequences.** The word $(s,t,s,t)$ is nonreduced: $\\ell(stst)=\\ell(ts)=2<4$, in agreement with the length formula $\\ell((st)^2)=\\min(4,2)=2$ of [[ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words]]. The prefix reflections of the element are $\\Phi(stst)=\\{sts,t\\}$, of cardinality $2=\\ell(stst)$, and $s\\notin\\Phi(stst)$ because $s=r_1=r_4$ occurs twice (an even number of times); deleting a different pair, e.g. the letters $s$ at positions $1$ and $3$, does not preserve the value: $t\\cdot t=1\\ne ts$.",
        "strategy": "Direct computation in $I_2(3)$ using the listed general facts.\n\n1.1 Exchange. The prefix reflection machinery is that of [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] (6); the exchange formula $s\\cdot st=t$ is an instance of part (2) of [[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] with $w=st$, $k=2$, $i=1$.\n2.1 The repeated prefix reflection. Compute the prefix reflections of the word $(s,t,s,t)$ directly from $r_i=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$ with $w_0=1$, $w_1=s$, $w_2=st$, $w_3=sts$: $r_1=s$, $r_2=sts$, $r_3=(st)s(ts)=t$ (using $(st)^3=1$, equivalently $sts=ts\\cdot t$), and $r_4=(sts)t(sts)=s$ (since $sts$ is an involution and conjugating $t$ by it returns $s$). Hence $r_1=r_4$, and part (6a) of [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] gives $stst=ts$ by deleting the two letters. The value identity is confirmed independently: $(st)^2=(st)^{-1}=ts$ because $st$ has order $3$ ([[ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words]] (1)).\n3.1 Consequences. The length assertion is the formula of [[ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words]] (3) for $k=2$, $m=3$; the reflection set $\\Phi$ is computed by part (6c) of [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] from the prefix reflections that occur an odd number of times, namely $r_2=sts$ and $r_3=t$; and $s$ occurs twice, so it is not in $\\Phi$ and its deletion is not licensed. The failed pair (1,3) is the check that only equal prefix reflections license a two-letter deletion.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (Princeton University Press 2008; author's complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
              "locator": "Lemma 3.2.6, printed pp. 31-32: if r_i = r_j the two letters can be deleted (the deletion condition (D))"
            },
